{"schema_version":1,"role":"independent-source-blind-mathematical-statement-decoder","decoder":"/root/fresh_blind_decoder50","packet_id":"ASTIS-BLIND-b0753416a337cc85","reconstructed_latex_or_text":"For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its norm, additive group, and Borel measurable structure (dimension zero is included), let V:E→ℝ, let α,β be nonnegative real numbers, and let η∈ℝ. Assume α>0, α≤β, V is globally C², and for every x,a∈E the genuine Hessian satisfies\n    α‖a‖² ≤ D²V(x)[a,a] ≤ β‖a‖².\nAssume also η>0 and βη≤1. These are the public mathematical hypotheses; the laws, disintegrations, Hilbert operators, regularity conclusions, and norm identities below are outputs.\n\nDefine μ as canonical volume tilted by −V. Thus its literal normalized density relative to volume is exp(−V(x))/∫ exp(−V(z)) dz, with the normalizer interpreted as the genuine Bochner integral. Let γ be the centered identity-covariance Gaussian on E. Define J as the pushforward of μ⊗γ by (x,z)↦(x,x+√η z). Write (X,Y) for these coordinates, so J is their literal joint law. Define ν=J.snd, the Y-marginal. Define Λ as J pushed forward by (x,y)↦(y,2x−y), and define F(x,y)=(x,2x−y).\n\nLet H=L²(J;ℝ), meaning the actual Hilbert space of J-almost-everywhere equivalence classes of real square-integrable functions on E×E. Let P:H→H be the actual L² conditional expectation projection onto the subspace measurable for the σ-algebra pulled back from the second coordinate, composed with its inclusion into H. In probabilistic notation this is conditional expectation given Y. P is fixed by this construction, not an existential or assumed certificate.\n\nThen μ,J,ν are probability measures. There exist two measurable kernels R,S:E→Prob(E), chosen once for all observations and all test functions, with the following properties. R is a conditional kernel for the swapped joint law J pushed forward by (x,y)↦(y,x): the actual composition product of that joint law's first marginal ν with R equals the swapped joint law. Thus R disintegrates X given Y in this measure identity sense. For every y∈E (not merely ν-almost every y),\n    S(y)=(x↦2x−y)_#R(y)\nand S(y) is the literal tilt of canonical volume by\n    u↦−V((y+u)/2)−‖y−u‖²/(8η).\nEquivalently S(y) has the displayed exponential density divided by its own genuine Bochner normalizer. Both R(y) and S(y) are probability measures at every y because R and S are Markov kernels. The actual first-marginal composition product Λ.fst⊗S equals Λ, and Λ.fst=ν. The every-y density and reflection identities are stronger in quantifier scope than a choice made only on an almost-everywhere set.\n\nFor the same R,S there exists a real linear isometry U:H→H. For every g∈H, its representative satisfies\n    (Ug)(x,y)=g(F(x,y))=g(x,2x−y) for J-almost every (x,y).\nU is involutive, meaning U(Ug)=g as a Hilbert-space class for every g, and its associated continuous linear map is self-adjoint. In the following expressions use that associated continuous linear map, and put\n    A=PUP,  B=(I−P)UP,  D=(I−P)U(I−P).\nProducts are composition of the actual continuous linear operators on H; star is the actual Hilbert adjoint. The following are exact operator equalities on H:\n    B*B=P−A²,\n    B*D=−AB*.\nFor every g∈H satisfying Pg=g, the actual Hilbert norms additionally satisfy\n    ‖Bg‖²=‖g‖²−‖Ag‖².\nThese statements concern the fixed P and the common U just constructed, rather than operators separately chosen for individual inputs.\n\nFor every real signed function f:E→ℝ which is globally C∞ and has compact support, define the actual function\n    Tf(y)=∫ f(u) S(y)(du).\nThe kernels and U have already been chosen before this universal test-function quantifier. Then Tf is genuinely differentiable on all of E. The functions f and Tf belong to L²(ν), and the E-valued gradient ∇Tf belongs to L²(ν). Here the gradient is the inverse Riesz image of the genuine real Fréchet derivative of Tf. The real function\n    y↦Var_{S(y)}(f)=∫(f(u)−∫f(v)S(y)(dv))² S(y)(du)\nis integrable with respect to ν.\n\nFor each such f there exists g∈H, allowed to depend on f, whose J-almost-everywhere representative is (x,y)↦f(y). This g satisfies Pg=g. The J-almost-everywhere representative of Ag is (x,y)↦Tf(y). Its norm and the norm of g obey the exact uncentered second-moment identities\n    ‖g‖²=∫f(y)² ν(dy),\n    ‖Ag‖²=∫Tf(y)² ν(dy).\nThe conditional variance integrates to the exact microscopic norm defect:\n    ∫Var_{S(y)}(f) ν(dy)=‖Bg‖².\nFinally the literal gradient energy of the differentiable function Tf obeys\n    η ∫‖∇Tf(y)‖² ν(dy)\n       ≤ [(1−αη)²/(4(1+αη))] ‖Bg‖².\nThe coefficient uses the given scalar α and η with exactly this normalization; β appears in the hypotheses and the constraint βη≤1, rather than explicitly in this coefficient.\n\nAll almost-everywhere representative equalities above are with respect to J; they do not identify pointwise derivatives of arbitrary representatives. Kernel identities indexed by y are explicitly for every y. All square-integrability, conditional-variance integrability, differentiability, probability, adjoint identities, and energy conclusions are produced under the stated hypotheses. The test-function conclusion is restricted to globally smooth compactly supported f; it does not itself assert an extension to arbitrary rough functions or a fractional-operator domain. No relation to an external source or theorem acceptance is asserted by this reconstruction.","reconstructed_theorem_text":"For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its norm, additive group, and Borel measurable structure (dimension zero is included), let V:E→ℝ, let α,β be nonnegative real numbers, and let η∈ℝ. Assume α>0, α≤β, V is globally C², and for every x,a∈E the genuine Hessian satisfies\n    α‖a‖² ≤ D²V(x)[a,a] ≤ β‖a‖².\nAssume also η>0 and βη≤1. These are the public mathematical hypotheses; the laws, disintegrations, Hilbert operators, regularity conclusions, and norm identities below are outputs.\n\nDefine μ as canonical volume tilted by −V. Thus its literal normalized density relative to volume is exp(−V(x))/∫ exp(−V(z)) dz, with the normalizer interpreted as the genuine Bochner integral. Let γ be the centered identity-covariance Gaussian on E. Define J as the pushforward of μ⊗γ by (x,z)↦(x,x+√η z). Write (X,Y) for these coordinates, so J is their literal joint law. Define ν=J.snd, the Y-marginal. Define Λ as J pushed forward by (x,y)↦(y,2x−y), and define F(x,y)=(x,2x−y).\n\nLet H=L²(J;ℝ), meaning the actual Hilbert space of J-almost-everywhere equivalence classes of real square-integrable functions on E×E. Let P:H→H be the actual L² conditional expectation projection onto the subspace measurable for the σ-algebra pulled back from the second coordinate, composed with its inclusion into H. In probabilistic notation this is conditional expectation given Y. P is fixed by this construction, not an existential or assumed certificate.\n\nThen μ,J,ν are probability measures. There exist two measurable kernels R,S:E→Prob(E), chosen once for all observations and all test functions, with the following properties. R is a conditional kernel for the swapped joint law J pushed forward by (x,y)↦(y,x): the actual composition product of that joint law's first marginal ν with R equals the swapped joint law. Thus R disintegrates X given Y in this measure identity sense. For every y∈E (not merely ν-almost every y),\n    S(y)=(x↦2x−y)_#R(y)\nand S(y) is the literal tilt of canonical volume by\n    u↦−V((y+u)/2)−‖y−u‖²/(8η).\nEquivalently S(y) has the displayed exponential density divided by its own genuine Bochner normalizer. Both R(y) and S(y) are probability measures at every y because R and S are Markov kernels. The actual first-marginal composition product Λ.fst⊗S equals Λ, and Λ.fst=ν. The every-y density and reflection identities are stronger in quantifier scope than a choice made only on an almost-everywhere set.\n\nFor the same R,S there exists a real linear isometry U:H→H. For every g∈H, its representative satisfies\n    (Ug)(x,y)=g(F(x,y))=g(x,2x−y) for J-almost every (x,y).\nU is involutive, meaning U(Ug)=g as a Hilbert-space class for every g, and its associated continuous linear map is self-adjoint. In the following expressions use that associated continuous linear map, and put\n    A=PUP,  B=(I−P)UP,  D=(I−P)U(I−P).\nProducts are composition of the actual continuous linear operators on H; star is the actual Hilbert adjoint. The following are exact operator equalities on H:\n    B*B=P−A²,\n    B*D=−AB*.\nFor every g∈H satisfying Pg=g, the actual Hilbert norms additionally satisfy\n    ‖Bg‖²=‖g‖²−‖Ag‖².\nThese statements concern the fixed P and the common U just constructed, rather than operators separately chosen for individual inputs.\n\nFor every real signed function f:E→ℝ which is globally C∞ and has compact support, define the actual function\n    Tf(y)=∫ f(u) S(y)(du).\nThe kernels and U have already been chosen before this universal test-function quantifier. Then Tf is genuinely differentiable on all of E. The functions f and Tf belong to L²(ν), and the E-valued gradient ∇Tf belongs to L²(ν). Here the gradient is the inverse Riesz image of the genuine real Fréchet derivative of Tf. The real function\n    y↦Var_{S(y)}(f)=∫(f(u)−∫f(v)S(y)(dv))² S(y)(du)\nis integrable with respect to ν.\n\nFor each such f there exists g∈H, allowed to depend on f, whose J-almost-everywhere representative is (x,y)↦f(y). This g satisfies Pg=g. The J-almost-everywhere representative of Ag is (x,y)↦Tf(y). Its norm and the norm of g obey the exact uncentered second-moment identities\n    ‖g‖²=∫f(y)² ν(dy),\n    ‖Ag‖²=∫Tf(y)² ν(dy).\nThe conditional variance integrates to the exact microscopic norm defect:\n    ∫Var_{S(y)}(f) ν(dy)=‖Bg‖².\nFinally the literal gradient energy of the differentiable function Tf obeys\n    η ∫‖∇Tf(y)‖² ν(dy)\n       ≤ [(1−αη)²/(4(1+αη))] ‖Bg‖².\nThe coefficient uses the given scalar α and η with exactly this normalization; β appears in the hypotheses and the constraint βη≤1, rather than explicitly in this coefficient.\n\nAll almost-everywhere representative equalities above are with respect to J; they do not identify pointwise derivatives of arbitrary representatives. Kernel identities indexed by y are explicitly for every y. All square-integrability, conditional-variance integrability, differentiability, probability, adjoint identities, and energy conclusions are produced under the stated hypotheses. The test-function conclusion is restricted to globally smooth compactly supported f; it does not itself assert an extension to arbitrary rough functions or a fractional-operator domain. No relation to an external source or theorem acceptance is asserted by this reconstruction.","reconstructed_text_sha256":"3bae63f552785537b8ea2116774fc2a8f52eb80e31d73474e4c36f99082b8205","semantic_slots":{"objects":{"space":"Finite-dimensional real inner-product space E, including dimension zero","potential":"V:E→ℝ","parameters":"α,β∈ℝ≥0 and η∈ℝ","laws":"μ=volume.tilted(−V); γ=stdGaussian(E); J=(x,z↦(x,x+√η z))_#(μ⊗γ); ν=J.snd; Λ=(x,y↦(y,2x−y))_#J","reflection":"F(x,y)=(x,2x−y)","hilbert_space":"H=L²(J;ℝ) of AE classes","projection":"P is actual second-coordinate conditional expectation followed by inclusion","kernels":"Common measurable Markov kernels R,S:E→E","isometry":"Common real linear isometry U:H→H with involution and self-adjoint continuous map","operators":"A=PUP; B=(I−P)UP; D=(I−P)U(I−P); products compose; star is Hilbert adjoint","test":"Globally C∞ compactly supported signed f:E→ℝ; Tf(y)=∫f dS(y)"},"hypotheses":["α>0","α≤β","V is globally C²","∀x,a∈E, α‖a‖²≤D²V(x)[a,a]≤β‖a‖²","η>0","βη≤1","E has finite-dimensional real inner-product and Borel structure"],"conclusion":["μ,J,ν are probability measures","R,S are Markov kernels at every y","(swap_#J).IsCondKernel(R), equivalently ν.compProd(R)=swap_#J","∀y, S(y)=(x↦2x−y)_#R(y)","∀y, S(y)=volume.tilted(u↦−V((y+u)/2)−‖y−u‖²/(8η))","Λ.IsCondKernel(S) and Λ.fst=ν","∀g∈H, Ug=g∘F J-AE","U²=I and U*=U","B*B=P−A² and B*D=−AB*","∀g∈H, Pg=g ⇒ ‖Bg‖²=‖g‖²−‖Ag‖²","For every smooth compactly supported f, Tf is differentiable, f,Tf,∇Tf are L²(ν), and y↦Var_{S(y)}f is L¹(ν)","For every such f, ∃g∈H with g=f∘snd J-AE, Pg=g, and Ag=Tf∘snd J-AE","‖g‖²=∫f²dν; ‖Ag‖²=∫Tf²dν; ∫Var_{S(y)}f dν=‖Bg‖²","η∫‖∇Tf‖²dν≤(1−αη)²/[4(1+αη)]·‖Bg‖²"],"dependence":"P is fixed by E,V,η and J. R,S may depend on the initial data but are chosen before all y, U and f. U is chosen after the common kernels and before all f. g is chosen separately after each admissible f. The energy constant depends explicitly on α,η; β imposes a hypothesis.","measures":"Canonical volume for tilted laws; centered identity-covariance γ for the product generating J; J for H and all AE representative identities; ν for all final L²/gradient/variance integrals; S(y) for inner conditional integrals. Λ is the (Y,2X−Y) law.","quantifiers":"Universal initial data satisfying all hypotheses; existential common R,S; every-y fiber identities; existential common U; every-g pullback, involution and norm-defect properties; universal smooth compact f; existential g dependent on f. P and A,B,D are lets with literal definitions.","regularity":"C² and Hessian bounds assumed for V. C∞ and compact support assumed only for each test f. Tf differentiability and all three MemLp conclusions, conditional variance integrability and probability measures are outputs. No extra higher derivatives of V, arbitrary rough test extension, or representative derivative equality is present.","domains_and_scopes":"All x,a,y,u∈E. H functions live on E×E modulo J-AE. Kernel reflection and density equalities hold for every y. Class pullback and g/Ag representatives hold only J-AE. Derivative and gradient assertions concern the literal Bochner-integral function Tf."},"uncertainties":null,"objects":{"space":"Finite-dimensional real inner-product space E, including 